 to physics and  wheeler created the wheeler-dewitt  equation so-called quantum cosmology the  wave function for quantum cosmological  origin that we've talked about on this  channel but in this context he worked  specifically after world war ii on  interactions between light and matter  and of course his star student richard  feynman went on to win the nobel prize  in 1965 for this discovery of the  interaction between light and matter  that we call quantum electrodynamics or  qet so the bright wheeler process is the  interconversion of photons particles of  light into matter into manifestations of  actual ordinary honest-to-goodness  matter and mass that could be then  predominantly it could be weighed it  could be interacted with and it could be  used to measure the strength of this  interaction between photonic matter and  ordinary matter one of the foundational  properties of modern physics are  conservation laws one such conservation  law is that you cannot create or destroy  energy therefore the energy the e in the  e equals m c squared equation has to go  into an equivalent amount of mass now  that mass can also not be of the form of  two like charges for the reason that  that would violate conservation  of charge so you couldn't create two  electrons or two positrons for example  they must come in pairs to cancel out  the two photons crashing together have  zero electrical charge together they  have zero net momentum so you must  create particles that are antimatter and  matter so namely an electron and a  positron the production of equal and  opposite amounts of matter and  antimatter along with the conservation  of momentum means that if you do produce  matter from pure energy from your pure  photons you could produce actual matter  antimatter pairs and this is exactly  what was measured by the rick  relativistic heavy ion collider in upton  new york which is on long island  brookhaven near where i was born almost  50 years ago  in this laboratory phenome